Titre$ Clé des Genres d'Ascomycètes
com$ Adaptation par Aurélien Paris
rem$ Nordic Macromycetes. Vol. 1 – Ascomycetes. Nordsvamp, Copenhagen, 309 p. (2000).

Key to the included orders of ascomycetes

1,0$ 1a : Frb absent ; parasitic, forming ± conspicuous galls on leaves and fruits of living trees and shrubs. Asci formed in a palisade within the host epidermis. $ Taphrinales (Taphrina). $s"Taphrinales (Taphrina)."
1,2$ 1b : Frb present or if not asci intermixed with paraphyses ; saprotrophic, parasitic or mycorrhizal.
2,3$ 2a : Frb an apothecium or a hypogeous, tuber-like cleistothecium.
2,8$ 2b : Frb a perithecium or a tiny, epigeous cleistothecium.
3,0$ 3a : Frb hypogeous, hollow at first, later filled with evanescent asci ; interior powdery at maturity, surface smooth to coarsely scaly. Sp brown, globose, ornamented. Ectotrophic. $ Eurotiales (Elaphomyces). $s"Eurotiales (Elaphomyces)."
3,4$ 3b : Frb an apothecium or a hypogeous tuber-like cleistothecium, solid at first and with persistent asci. Sp versiform, smooth or ornamented ; interior never powdery at maturity ; sapro- or ectotrophic.
4,CS14$ 4a : Asci operculate in taxa with active spore dispersal. Frb a short-lived apothecium, a tuber-like cleistothecium with passive spore dispersal aided by animals or rarely absent. Sp often large, ellipsoid to globose and often ornamented ; ascus walls amyloid or non-amyloid. Many soil and dung inhabiting saprophitic species, but some ectotrophic. $ Pezizales. $s"Pezizales."
4,5$ 4b : Asci inoperculate. Frb a short- or long-lived apothecium. Sp tiny to large, of various form ; ascus walls non-amyloid, apical apparatus amyloid or non-amyloid. Saprotrophic, parasitic or rarely ectotrophic.
5,CS25$ 5a : Frb formed within host tissue or within a black sclerotium-like stroma formed within host tissue, variously shaped, often slit-like or splitting host surface in a stellate or irregular fashion ; in some spp. the apothecia develop in spring on overwintered stromata. Sp phaseoliform to filiform, 1- to multi-celled ; ascus apical structure poroid ; paraphyses much branched apically or curled at the tip. Saprophitic or parasitic. $ Rhytismatales (Rhytismataceae). $s"Rhytismatales (Rhytismataceae)."
5,6$ 5b : Frb formed outside host tissue or erumpent, cup-, disc-, club-shaped or resembling a corticioid fungus. Sp of various form.
6,0$ 6a : Paraphyses and croziers absent. Frb clavate. Ascus wall amyloid after KOH treatment ; sp reniform, producing phialoconidia, hyaline ; ascus apex thickened, with narrow pore. Associated with conifer roots (parasitic ?). $ Neolectales (Neolecta). $s"Neolectales (Neolecta)."
6,7$ 6b : Paraphyses present, if absent then frb tiny, with very few asci. Frb variously shaped. Ascus wall non-amyloid ; apical apparatus absent or pore-like, amyloid or non-amyloid.
7,CS1$ 7a : Asci dehiscing with a slit, without operculum or internal pore-like structure, sometimes with subapical annular thickening, saccate, from 1- to often polysporous ; sp ellipsoid, smooth, hyaline. Frb simple apothecia or hardly developed in one genus, short-lived ; coprophilous saprotrophs. $ Erysiphales (Thelebolaceae). $s"Erysiphales (Thelebolaceae)."
7,CS5$ 7b : Asci not dehiscing slit-like, often with distinct internal pore-like structure ; sp ellipsoid, fusoid or filiform. Frb discoid, cupulate, turbinate, clavate, etc., often relatively long lived and firm textured, some develop from sclerotia ; many litter inhabiting species, saprotrophic, parasitic or rarely ectotrophic. $ Leotiales. $s"Leotiales."
8,0$ 8a : Frb a stipitate cleistothecium. Asci spherical, deliquesching. Saprotrophs on hoofs, horns and feathers. $ Onygenales (Onygena). $s"Onygenales (Onygena)."
8,9$ 8b : Frb a perithecium, either solitary or enclosed in stroma. Asci unitunicate, mostly cylindric, persistent, typically with an apical apparatus. On a range of substrates but not on hoofs, horns and feathers.
9,CS2$ 9a : Frb never carbonized, often with brown, yellow or red colours ; stroma when present soft and fleshy. Paraphyses not present at maturity ; asci with non-amyloid apical apparatus ; sp often hyaline to pale brown, ornamented and 2-celled, never with germ apparatus. Saprotrophic or parasitic on plants, insects or fungi. $ Hypocreales. $s"Hypocreales."
9,10$ 9b : Frb carbonized or if not then maturing in living leaves ; stroma, when present, mostly with firm texture. True paraphyses present, asci with amyloid or non-amyloid apical apparatus ; sp hyaline to opaque brown, smooth or ornamented, often with appendages and some with a pore or slit-like germ apparatus. Saprotrophic or parasitic on plants.
10,0$ 10a : Asci typically very long stipitate with allantoid sp arranged more or less biseriately or irregularly ; apical apparatus diminutive, amyloid or nor ; anamorphs typically coelomycetous. $ Diatrypales (Diatrype). $s"Diatrypales (Diatrype)."
10,11$ 10b : Asci not long stipitate or sp shaped differently.
11,CS24$ 11a : Parasitic on leaves of Rosaceae ; stroma immersed beneath epidermis in living leaves, in shades of orange. Asci clavate ; sp ellipsoid, 1-celled, smooth, hyaline to yellowish. Stromata harden and blacken after leaf fall and the perithecia are fully mature in spring. $ Phyllachorales (Polystigma). $s"Phyllachorales (Polystigma)."
11,12$ 11b : Not parasitic on Rosaceae and with orange stromata.
12,0$ 12a : Sp inconspicuously terminally bi-porous, 1-celled, smoky grey or olive ; ascus apparatus minute, non-amyloid. Perithecia aggregated in small to very big, ± erumpent stromata, arranged mono- or polysticuously. Saprotrophic or parasitic on deciduous trees and conifers. $ Sordariales (Camarops). $s"Sordariales (Camarops)."
12,13$ 12b : Sp not terminally bi-porous, 1-celled or with hyaline dwarf cell (primary appendage) ; apical apparatus amyloid, dextrinoid or unstained.
13,CS24$ 13a : Sp hyaline to yellowish without germ apparatus ; apical apparatus inamyloid. Frb immersed beneath blackened clypeus. Anamorphs coelomycetous. Parasitic on herbs including grasses (note that a range of pyrenomycetes, e.g. belonging to the Diaporthales will key out here, but they are not included in this work). $ Phyllachorales (Phyllachora). $s"Phyllachorales (Phyllachora)."
13,CS26$ 13b : Sp grey, olive brown or nearly black, with longitudinal germ slits, rarely without or with poroid, non-terminal germ apparatus ; apical apparatus normally amyloid or dextrinoid. Stromata present, typically as flattened or pulvinate crusts, or erect club-shaped or filiform ; when stroma clypeoid then sp either brown and/or apical apparatus amyloid ; anamorphs when present usually hyphomycetous. Saprotrophic or more rarely necrotrophic or parasitic. $ Xylariales (Xylariaceae). $s"Xylariales (Xylariaceae)."

CS1,0$ Erysiphales GWYNNER-VAUGHAN - Thelebolaceae (BRUMM.) ECKBLAD

1,0$ 1a : Asci with 8-64 sp, sp 11.5-13 x 3.5-6 µm. $ Ascozonus. $s"Ascozonus." 
1,2$ 1b : Asci with more sp and/or sp smaller.
2,3$ 2a : Asci with 8 sp.
2,4$ 2b : Asci with 32-7000 sp.
3,0$ 3a : Apothecia c. 0.1 mm, without excipulum. $ Pseudoascozonus. $s"Pseudoascozonus."
3,0$ 3b : Apothecia c. 0.5-1 mm, with excipulum. $ Thelebolus p.p. $s"Thelebolus p.p."
4,0$ 4a : Apothecia with stiff, pointed hairs. $ Trichobolus. $s"Trichobolus."
4,0$ 4b : Apothecia glabrous. $ Thelebolus. $s"Thelebolus."

CS2,0$ Hypocreales LINDAU

1,CS3$ 1a : Sp filiform or almost so, mostly multiseptate ; asci with distinct apical crown. $ Clavicipitaceae. $s"Clavicipitaceae."
1,CS4$ 1b : Sp globose, ellipsoid to cylindric, 1-septate or more rarely aseptate or with several septa ; asci with simple apical pores or without apparent apical apparatus. $ Hypocreaceae. $s"Hypocreaceae."

CS3,0$ Clavicipitaceae (LINDAU) EARLE ex ROGERSON

1,2$ 1a : Parasitic on fungi incl. lichenized fungi and insects.
1,3$ 1b : Parasitic on inflorescences of Poaceae and Cyperaceae.
2,0$ 2a : Parasitic on Bertia moriformis, Claviceps-sclerotia or Cladonia-thalli ; perithecia united or not in fleshy stromata. $ Neobarya. $s"Neobarya."
2,0$ 2b : Parasitic on insects or Elaphomyces ; perithecia united in fleshy stromata. $ Cordyceps. $s"Cordyceps."
3,0$ 3a : Stromata sheathing living culms of Poaceae in late summer ; first white and anamorphic then straw coloured with immersed Perithecia. $ Epichloe. $s"Epichloe."
3,0$ 3b : Perithecia immersed in capitate, purplish, stipitate stromata, developed from sclerotia in early summer ; sclerotia developed in late summer and autumn. $ Claviceps. $s"Claviceps."

CS4,0$ Hypocreaceae DE NOT. s.l.

1,0$ 1a : Frb a cleistothecium containing orange sp in globose asci. $ Heleococcum. $s"Heleococcum."
1,2$ 1b : Frb a perithecium, non-stromatic or seated upon or contained within a stroma or subiculum. Asci clavate to cylindric, sp of various coulours or hyaline.
2,3$ 2a : Sp with more than one septum.
2,10$ 2b : Sp without or with one septum.
3,4$ 3a : Sp muriform.
3,5$ 3b : Sp only transversely septate.
4,0$ 4a : Sp without cilia. Perithecia in dense clusters on Berberis or Ribes. $ Nectria p.p. $s"Nectria p.p."
4,0$ 4b : Sp ciliate. Perithecia superficial on lichen-thalli. $ Paranectria p.p. $s"Paranectria p.p."
5,0$ 5a : Perithecia appearing black but dark blue in transmitted light. $ Gibberella. $s"Gibberella."
5,6$ 5b : Perithecia in shades of yellow and red, never black or blue.
6,0$ 6a : Perithecia with hyaline, thick-walled hairs. $ Trichonectria. $s"Trichonectria."
6,7$ 6b : Perithecia not with such hairs.
7,0$ 7a : On conifer bark ; perithecia conspicuous, red. $ Nectria p.p. $s"Nectria p.p."
7,8$ 7b : On leaf litter, lichen- and algae-thalli, etc.
8,0$ 8a : On leaf litter, rarely on woody substrates. Sp 3-5-septate, 40-80 µm long. Perithecia superficial. $ Calonectria. $s"Calonectria."
8,9$ 8b : On lichen- or algae-thalli. Perithecia immersed or superficial.
9,0$ 9a : Sp without cilia. Perithecia completely immersed, only ostiole erumpent. On lichen- or algae-thalli. $ Pronectria p.p. $s"Pronectria p.p."
9,0$ 9b : Sp ciliate. Perithecia superficial. On lichen-thalli. $ Paranectria p.p. $s"Paranectria p.p."
10,0$ 10a : Sp non-septate, fusoid, thick-walled, smooth, more than 40 µm long. On dung. $ Selinia. $s"Selinia."
10,11$ 10b : Sp septate or if non-septate with other shapes or ornamentations. Not on dung.
11,12$ 11a : Sp at maturity breaking into part sp.
11,13$ 11b : Sp not breaking into part sp at maturity.
12,0$ 12a : Part sp acutely pointed to apiculate. Stromata arachnoid, very thin. $ Arachnocrea. $s"Arachnocrea."
12,0$ 12b : Part sp normally with rounded ends. Stromata spongy and loose to firm with distinct rind. $ Hypocrea. $s"Hypocrea."
12,0,N$ See also Podostroma.
13,0$ 13a : Stromata with fingerlike outgrowths. Parasitic on Hymenochaete. $ Hypocreopsis. $s"Hypocreopsis."
13,14$ 13b : Stromata either absent, pulvinate or effused without finger-like outgrowths. Not associated with Hymenochaete.
14,15$ 14a : Stromata developing on fungi or on a thin subiculum. Sp ± fusoid.
14,17$ 14b : Stromata, if present, developing on other substrate or sp with different shape.
15,0$ 15a : Sp > 15 µm, densely verrucose, often with distinct apiculi ; anamorphs in Cladobotryum, Mycogone, Papulospora, Pseudohansfordia, Sepedonium and Sibirina. $ Hypomyces. $s"Hypomyces."
15,16$ 15b : Sp < 15 µm long, never apiculate.
16,0$ 16a : On both myxomycetes and fungi ; anamorphs Acremonium-like. $ Nectriopsis. $s"Nectriopsis."
16,0$ 16b : On Stereum, anamorphs in Gliocladium. $ Sphaerostilbella. $s"Sphaerostilbella."
17,18$ 17a : Perithecia superficial, with or without a basal stroma.
17,20$ 17b : Perithecia immersed, erumpent or not.
18,0$ 18a : Perithecia hyaline or whitish. On Hylocomium. $ Bryonectria. $s"Bryonectria."
18,19$ 18b : Perithecia more colourful or host different.
19,0$ 19a : Perithecia smooth or scurfy, rarely hairy, stromata often developed underneath. Sp 2-celled. On all kind of substrates. $ Nectria p.p. $s"Nectria p.p."
19,0$ 19b : Perithecia more or less hairy, no obvious stroma present. Sp aseptate. On leaves of Buxus. $ Pseudonectria. $s"Pseudonectria."
20,0$ 20a : On lichenized fungi and Laminaria-thalli. $ Pronectria. $s"Pronectria."
20,0$ 20b : On other substrates. $ Nectriella. $s"Nectriella."

CS5,0$ Leotiales S.E.CARP.

1,CS6$ 1a : Frb a very thin, whitish mat, a few mm in diam. On bark (often inner side) of Pinus. $ Ascocorticiaceae. $s"Ascocorticiaceae."
1,2$ 1b : Frb with a more distinct shape.
2,0$ 2a : Sp hyaline, filiform, very long, breaking into numerous part-sp, 1-2.5 µm thick. In springs. $ Vibrisseaceae (Vibrissea). $s"Vibrisseaceae (Vibrissea)."
2,3$ 2b : Sp hyaline or coloured ; if very long then not breaking apart or thicker than 3 µm.
3,4$ 3a : Apothecia arising from a stroma or sclerotium, or from stromatised host tissue.
3,5$ 3b : Apothecia not with the above characters.
4,CS13$ 4a : Apothecia cupulate, arising distinct sclerotia or stroma with a well differentiated rind and medulla. Obligatory or facultative parasites. Sp unicellular. $ Sclerotiniaceae. $s"Sclerotiniaceae."
4,CS12$ 4b : Apothecia cupulate or campanulate, arising from an indeterminate brown to black crust-like stroma or stromatal host tissue with or without a rind. Mainly saprotrophs. Sp uni- or multicellular. $ Rutstroemiaceae. $s"Rutstroemiaceae."
5,CS9$ 5a : Apothecia subsessile or stipitate, disc-shaped or cup-shaped ; outside covered with white or coloured hairs. On leaves, herbaceous stems or wood. $ Hyaloscyphaceae. $s"Hyaloscyphaceae."
5,6$ 5b : Apothecia not with the above combinations of characters.
6,7$ 6a : Frb distinctly stipitate.
6,9$ 6b : Frb sessile or subsessile.
7,CS10$ 7a : Apothecia convex and lobed, clavate, hemispheric or globose, or disc-shaped to cup-shaped, margin free of stem. $ Leotiaceae. $s"Leotiaceae."
7,8$ 7b : Apothecia clavate, spathulate or capitate, with no abrupt or definite margin between hymenium and stem.
8,CS8$ 8a : Paraphyses present. $ Geoglossaceae. $s"Geoglossaceae."
8,CS$ 8b : Paraphyses absent. Frb with white to yellowish stem and yellow hymenium. $ Neolectales. $s"Neolectales."
9,CS7$ 9a : Apothecia disc-shaped, margin even or torn, outside often dark brownish. Flesh soft, composed of angular or subglobose cells. $ Dermateaceae. $s"Dermateaceae."
9,10$ 9b : Apothecia disc-shaped, cup-shaped or turbinate ; margin even or delicately dentate ; outside often concolorous with the hymenium. Flesh tough, leathery or gelatinous.
10,CS11$ 10a : Frb disc-shaped, sessile, 1-2 mm, waxy, light-coloured and hyaline when fresh. Asci and paraphyses adherent ; paraphyses capitate. $ Orbiliaceae. $s"Orbiliaceae."
10,CS10$ 10b : Frb variously shaped, sessile or stipitate, small or large, rarely hyaline. Asci and paraphyses not adherent. $ Leotiaceae. $s"Leotiaceae."

CS6,0$ Ascocorticiaceae J.SCHRÖT.

1,0$ 1a : Sp ellipsoid, aseptate ; asci cylindric, 8-spored. $ Ascocorticium. $s"Ascocorticium."
1,0$ 1b : Sp vermiform, 3-septate ; asci broadly clavate, 16-spored. $ Ascocorticiellum. $s"Ascocorticiellum."

CS7,0$ Dermateaceae FR.

1,0$ 1a : On soil or plant debris. Apothecia turbinate to cup-shaped, hymenium bright yellow ; margin dentate ; outside dark brown to black. $ Podophacidium. $s"Podophacidium."
1,0$ 1b : On wood of deciduous trees. Apothecia disc-shaped, hymenium greyish or yellowish grey, margin nearly white, even ; outside brownish. $ Mollisia. $s"Mollisia."

CS8,0$ Geoglossaceae CORDA

1,2$ 1a : Frb clavate, black or brown.
1,5$ 1b : Frb clavate or compressed, yellow, ochraceous or green.
2,3$ 2a : Sp persistently hyaline.
2,4$ 2b : Sp brown at maturity.
3,0$ 3a : Frb black or dark purplish. $ Geoglossum. $s"Geoglossum."
3,0$ 3b : Frb pale brown to reddish brown. $ Microglossum. $s"Microglossum."
4,0$ 4a : Setae absent. $ Geoglossum. $s"Geoglossum."
4,0$ 4b : Brown setae present (frb hairy, use lens !). $ Trichoglossum. $s"Trichoglossum."
5,0$ 5a : Frb ± green. $ Microglossum. $s"Microglossum."
5,6$ 5b : Frb yellow or ochraceous.
6,7$ 6a : Sp filiform, finally septate.
6,8$ 6b : Sp fusoid.
7,0$ 7a : Frb spathulate, with laterally compressed hymenium. $ Spathularia. $s"Spathularia."
7,0$ 7b : Frb capitate, with rounded hymenium. $ Cudonia. $s"Cudonia."
8,0$ 8a : Stem white, ± hyaline, often > 1 mm thick ; hymenium bright yellow to orange yellow. In water or in very moist places. $ Mitrula. $s"Mitrula."
8,0$ 8b : Stem pale, not hyaline, often < 1 mm thick ; hymenium pale yellow to pale orange yellow. In mosses in forests and open land. $ Bryoglossum. $s"Bryoglossum."

CS9,0$ Hyaloscyphaceae NANNF.

1,2$ 1a : Frb seated on an arachnoid subiculum.
1,4$ 1b : Frb not seated on a subiculum.
2,0$ 2a : Sp septate at maturity. Outside of frb white, yellow or orange. $ Arachnopeziza p.p. $s"Arachnopeziza p.p."
2,3$ 2b : Sp aseptate. Outside of frb olivaceous to dark brown.
3,0$ 3a : Subiculum copious. $ Eriopezia. $s"Eriopezia."
3,0$ 3b : Subiculum scanty. $ Proliferodiscus. $s"Proliferodiscus."
4,5$ 4a : Hairs at least partially rough or densely granulose, or > 100 µm long.
4,18$ 4b : Hairs smooth or with scattered granules, < 100 µm long.
5,6$ 5a : Hairs sharply pointed.
5,7$ 5b : Hairs cylindric or tapering, but not sharply pointed.
6,0$ 6a : Hairs multiseptate. $ Albotricha. $s"Albotricha."
6,0$ 6b : Hairs 0-2-septate. $ Incrupila. $s"Incrupila."
7,8$ 7a : Hairs at least partially rough.
7,12$ 7b : Hairs smooth or with superficial granules.
8,0$ 8a : Apical 1-3 cell(s) of hairs smooth. $ Dasyscyphella. $s"Dasyscyphella."
8,9$ 8b : Hairs granulose at apex.
9,0$ 9a : Hairs smooth in basal part, ± clavate, to 50 µm long. $ Cistella. $s"Cistella."
9,10$ 9b : Hairs entirely rough, cylindric, often longer than 50 µm.
10,0$ 10a : Frb revivable. Paraphyses mostly cylindric. On coniferous wood. $ Lachnellula. $s"Lachnellula."
10,11$ 10b : Frb ephemeral. Paraphyses cylindric or lanceolate. On various substrates.
11,0$ 11a : Hairs brown. Paraphyses cylindric. $ Fuscolachnum. $s"Fuscolachnum."
11,0$ 11b : Hairs hyaline, if brown then paraphyses distinctly lanceolate. $ Lachnum. $s"Lachnum."
12,0$ 12a : Outside blackish with greyish hairs. $ Proliferodiscus. $s"Proliferodiscus."
12,13$ 12b : Outside with brown, brownish or white hairs.
13,0$ 13a : Ascus pore non-amyloid. $ Perrotia. $s"Perrotia."
13,14$ 13b : Ascus pore amyloid.
14,15$ 14a : On herbaceous stems.
14,16$ 14b : On wood.
15,0$ 15a : Hairs hyaline, yellowish or ochraceous. $ Trichopeziza. $s"Trichopeziza."
15,0$ 15b : Hairs brown. $ Lasiobelonium p.p. $s"Lasiobelonium p.p."
16,0$ 16a : Hairs densely incrusted with amorphous granules. $ Neodasyscypha. $s"Neodasyscypha."
16,17$ 16b : Hairs smooth or with scattered granules.
17,0$ 17a : Hairs thin-walled. $ Arachnopeziza p.p. $s"Arachnopeziza p.p."
17,0$ 17b : Hairs with thickened walls. $ Lasiobelonium p.p. $s"Lasiobelonium p.p."
18,0$ 18a : Paraphyses richly branched. $ Polydesmia. $s"Polydesmia."
18,19$ 18b : Paraphyses simple or sparsely branched.
19,20$ 19a : Hairs with thick and glassy walls ; lumen narrow or absent.
19,23$ 19b : Hairs thin-walled or slightly thick-walled.
20,0$ 20a : Paraphyses with hooked apices. $ Unguiculella. $s"Unguiculella."
20,21$ 20b : Paraphyses without hooked apices.
21,0$ 21a : Hairs loosening glassiness in > 5 % KOH. $ Urceolella. $s"Urceolella."
21,22$ 21b : Hairs retaining glassiness in KOH.
22,0$ 22a : Hairs purplish in Melzer. $ Unguicularia. $s"Unguicularia."
22,0$ 22b : Hairs not purplish in Melzer. $ Hyalopeziza. $s"Hyalopeziza."
23,0$ 23a : Hairs uncinate or helicoids, apex mostly blunt, not forked. $ Hamatocanthoscypha. $s"Hamatocanthoscypha."
23,24$ 23b : Hairs straight or with a strongly bent and sharply pointed apex, or some hairs with forked apex.
24,25$ 24a : Hairs cylindric.
24,26$ 24b : Hairs tapering or with a very narrow apical part.
25,0$ 25a : Apothecia white to yellow. $ Psilachnum. $s"Psilachnum."
25,0$ 25b : Apothecia at least basally brown. $ Microscypha. $s"Microscypha."
26,0$ 26a : Apothecia dark coloured. $ Dematioscypha. $s"Dematioscypha."
26,27$ 26b : Apothecia with pale or bright colours.
27,0$ 27a : On wood, cones or old polypores. $ Hyaloscypha p.p. $s"Hyaloscypha p.p."
27,28$ 27b : On leaves, ferns or herbaceous stems.
28,0$ 28a : Apex of hairs strongly curved or irregularly bent. $ Phialina. $s"Phialina."
28,29$ 28b : Hairs conical with apex tapering to a narrow point.
29,0$ 29a : Paraphyses lanceolate, far exceeding the asci. On ferns. $ See Albotricha washingtonensis.
29,0$ 29b : Paraphyses filiform, not exceeding the asci. On various substrates. $ Hyaloscypha p.p. $s"Hyaloscypha p.p."

CS10,0$ Leotiaceae CORDA

1,2$ 1a : On bryophytes or in Sphagnum on stems of Cyperaceae.
1,6$ 1b : On other substrates.
2,0$ 2a : In Sphagnum on stems of Cyperaceae. Apothecia 0.4-2 cm, purplish. $ See Ascocoryne turficola.
2,3$ 2b : On bryophytes.
3,0$ 3a : Apothecia cylindric, egg-shaped or campanulate ; frb stipitate. $ See Bryoglossum.
3,4$ 3b : Apothecia cup- or disc-shaped ; frb stipitate or sessile.
4,0$ 4a : Apothecia dark bluish green. $ Mniaecia. $s"Mniaecia."
4,5$ 4b : Apothecia brown or brownish.
5,0$ 5a : On Sphagnum. Sp 14-17 x 3-4 µm, ± ellipsoid, aseptate. $ See Hymenoscyphus vasaensis.
5,0$ 5b : On other mosses. Sp 6-10 µm broad, ± fusoid or rhomboid, often septate at maturity. $ Bryoscyphus. $s"Bryoscyphus."
6,7$ 6a : Frb terrestrial.
6,10$ 6b : Frb not terrestrial.
7,0$ 7a : Apothecia substipitate. $ Discinella. $s"Discinella."
7,8$ 7b : Frb distinctly stipitate.
8,0$ 8a : Sp 0-1-septate. Apothecia sphaeric to hemisphaeric. $ Sarcoleotia. $s"Sarcoleotia."
8,9$ 8b : Sp 5- to multiseptate. Apothecia convex, folded or lobed with recurved margin.
9,0$ 9a : Apothecia gelatinous. $ Leotia. $s"Leotia."
9,0$ 9b : Apothecia not gelatinous. $ See Cudonia.
10,11$ 10a : Sp filiform and > 30 µm long.
10,15$ 10b : Sp not filiform, mostly shorter.
11,12$ 11a : Apothecia at first closed, opening when mature.
11,13$ 11b : Apothecia not so.
12,0$ 12a : Asci non-amyloid. Apothecia opening irregularly, with crenulate edges. $ Discocainia. $s"Discocainia."
12,0$ 12b : Asci with amyloid pore. Apothecia opening regularly. $ Godronia. $s"Godronia."
13,0$ 13a : Disc black. On Tilia. $ Holwaya. $s"Holwaya."
13,14$ 13b : Disc pale or greenish. On wood in water or on conifers.
14,0$ 14a : On wood or stems soaked in water. $ See Vibrisseaceae.
14,0$ 14b : On coniferous wood in more dry habitats. $ Gorgoniceps. $s"Gorgoniceps."
15,16$ 15a : Frb with a long stem and a well-delimited globose, cylindric or campanulate apothecium.
15,17$ 15b : Frb sessile or apothecia with a different shape.
16,0$ 16a : On plant debris in lemming burrows. $ Capillipes. $s"Capillipes."
