CS13,0$ Sclerotiniaceae WHETZEL

1,2$ 1a : Apothecia arising from a distinct sclerotium with a well differentiated rind and medulla.
1,6$ 1b : Apothecia arising from an indeterminate, crust-like, black stroma or from stromatized host tissues.
2,3$ 2a : Sclerotia tuberoid to globose, borne in soil between roots of the host ; sclerotial medulla without obvious remnants of host tissues, or without gelatinous substance between sclerotial hyphae.
2,4$ 2b : Sclerotia cylindric, loaf-shaped, tuberoid, pyramidal or discoid ; on stems, leaves or roots of herbaceous plants ; rarely borne inside culms or stems.
3,0$ 3a : Outer excipulum with a layer of prismatic, elongate cells embedded in a gelatinous matrix. Macroconidial state unknown. $ Dumontinia. $s"Dumontinia."
3,0$ 3b : Outer excipulum with a layer of angular to globose cells, no gelatinous matrix. Macroconidial state unknown. $ Sclerotinia. $s"
4,0$ 4a : Sclerotia borne within stems of juncaceous and cyperaceous hosts, sclerotia cylindric to tuberoid. Macroconidial state unknown (see also Sclerotinia tetraspora). $ Myriosclerotinia. $s"Myriosclerotinia."
4,5$ 4b : Sclerotia formed on leaves, stems and roots of other plants, wholly or partially embedded in and digesting host tissues ; sclerotial medulla with remnants of host tissues, or with a gelatinous substance between sclerotial hyphae.
5,0$ 5a : Sclerotia discoid ; on wilting flowers (petals) of Rhododendron. Macroconidia borne on short conidiophores (form genus Ovulitis). $ Ovulinia. $s"Ovulinia."
5,0$ 5b : Sclerotia pyramidal, discoid or flat ; on stems, leaves or rhizomes. Macroconidia borne on straight, branched conidiophores (form genus Botrytis). $ Botryotinia. $s"Botryotinia."
6,7$ 6a : Stroma a black crust-like stroma with a well differentiated rind.
6,9$ 6b : Stroma consisting of areas of stromatized (blackened) host tissues with or without an obvious stromatal rind.
7,0$ 7a : Stroma crusty, black, mummified, formed within fruits of ericaceous and rosaceous hosts. Macroconidial state on leaves or fruits ; macroconidia borne in monilioid chains separated by disjunctors or not (form genus Monilia). $ Monilinia. $s"Monilinia."
7,8$ 7b : Stroma not as above.
8,0$ 8a : Stroma a blackened, mantling stroma of indefinite dimensions developed on rhizomes and bulbs of monocotyledons. Macroconidial state absent. $ Stromatinia. $s"Stromatinia."
8,0$ 8b : Stroma an indefinite, crust-like stroma ; on leaves of Vaccinium myrtillus. Conidial anamorph with four-armed diasporas (form genus Valdensia), ca. 0.5 mm diam. $ Valdensinia. $s"Valdensinia."
9,0$ 9a : Sp ± allantoid. On wood. $ Encoelia. $s"Encoelia."
9,10$ 9b : Sp ± ellipsoid. On leaves, catkins, fruits, bulbils.
10,0$ 10a : Apothecia short-stipitate, at first closed and ‘cup-shaped’, then expanding and radially splitting in a stellate manner, hymenium pale yellowish brown, outside brownish black ; on leaves and branchlets. Macroconidial state borne in pycnidia-like organs, consisting of arthroconidia in chains on branches on the host (form genus Acarosporium). $ Pycnopeziza. $s"Pycnopeziza."
10,0$ 10b : Apothecia regularly cup-shaped, hymenium and receptacle concolorous. $ Ciboria. $s"Ciboria."

CS14,0$ Pezizales BESSEY

1,2$ 1a : Frb in soil, hypogeous or nearly so, closed or with one to several small apical openings. Spore liberation passive.
1,7$ 1b : Frb epigeous ; on soil, wood or dung. At maturity with free hymenium or asci protruding. Spore liberation active.
2,3$ 2a : Frb solid, ± regularly tuber-like, with veined gleba and well defined peridium, with or without rhizoids on the lower side.
2,5$ 2b : Frb ± hollow, often strongly folded and with several smaller or larger interior lacunes, in some spp. with a small, central opening on the upper side, often irregular in shape and/or with thin, ill defined peridium.
3,CS19$ 3a : Frb with small depression on the upper side and rhizoids on the lower side, usually < 2 cm. Asci amyloid, at least at apex, 8-spored ; sp ornamented. $ Pezizaceae (Pachyphloeus). $s"Pezizaceae (Pachyphloeus)"
3,4$ 3b : Frb without specific depression on the upper side, with or without rhizoids on the lower side, often > 2 cm. Asci non-amyloid, 1-8-spored, sp ornamented or smooth.
4,CS17$ 4a : Sp ellipsoid and smooth or > 50 µm long and verrucose. $ Helvellaceae. $s"Helvellaceae."
4,CS23$ 4b : Sp ornamented and if > 50 µm long then with a reticulate ornamentation. $ Tuberaceae. $s"Tuberaceae."
5,CS17$ 5a : Frb grey, pubescent, mostly epigeous, closed, ± hollow and strongly folded inside. Sp ellipsoid, smooth. $ Helvellaceae (Helvella astieri). $s"Helvellaceae (Helvella astieri)."
5,6$ 5b : Frb different.
6,CS20$ 6a : Sp smooth or if ornamented then frb ± cup-shaped with a central opening on top and folded-hollow inside. $ Pyronemataceae p.p. $s"Pyronemataceae p.p."
6,CS16$ 6b : Sp ornamented. Frb with very irregular outline or strongly folded inside, reddish brown or becoming red when sectioned. $ Discinaceae p.p. $s"Discinaceae p.p."
7,8$ 7a : Asci amyloid, at least at apex.
7,9$ 7b : Asci non-amyloid.
8,CS15$ 8a : Apothecia whitish, yellowish, greyish, with bluish or purplish tinge, usually 0.1-12 mm broad, pulvinate, disc-shaped or turbinate. Sp hyaline or purplish, globose or ellipsoid, smooth or ornamented, without guttules. Often on dung. $ Ascobolaceae. $s"Ascobolaceae."
8,CS19$ 8b : Apothecia greyish, brownish, greenish, bluish, purplish or black, usually 1-8 cm broad, pulvinate, disc-shaped or cup-shaped. Sp hyaline to pale brownish, globose, ellipsoid or fusoid, smooth or ornamented, with or without guttules. $ Pezizaceae p.p. $s"Pezizaceae p.p."
9,CS21$ 9a : Apothecia either sessile, orange and on needles or twigs of Juniperus, Abies or Picea, or stipitate with scarlet hymenium and on ± buried twigs and branches. Spring. $ Sarcoscyphaceae. $s"Sarcoscyphaceae."
9,10$ 9b : Apothecia not with the above combination of characters.
10,CS22$ 10a : Apothecia either 0.5-12 cm broad, with dark brown to black hymenium, cup-shaped, subglobose, infundibuliform or urceolate, with greyish, brownish to black, glabrous, wrinkled to folded or hairy outside or apothecia 0.1-0.3 cm broad, whitish, disc-shaped with long brown hairs, on needles of Pinus. Early spring to summer. $ Sarcosomataceae. $s"Sarcosomataceae."
10,11$ 10b : Apothecia not with the above combination of characters.
11,0$ 11a : On dung of carnivorous or herbivorous animals or birds. Apothecia whitish, yellowish or brownish, with a distinct excipulum. Asci with 8-7000 hyaline sp. $ See Thelebolaceae.
11,12$ 11b : Apothecia not with the above combination of characters.
12,13$ 12a : Apothecia from early spring to early summer, one spp. in autumn ; often stipitate or substipitate, several cm high and broad, often thick-fleshed (> 2 mm), yellow brown, brown to blackish brown. Sp with prominent appendages at the poles or smooth and globose or ellipsoid. On soil or wood.
12,14$ 12b : Apothecia in autumn or if in spring then smaller, of another colour and more thin-fleshed (< 2 mm).
13,CS18$ 13a : Most spp. with a campanulate, even or wrinkled, sponge-like or honeycomb-like ‘cap’ and a ± hollow, whitish stem, longer than cap, one sp. substipitate, cup-shaped, fragile, 3-12 cm broad and with a smell of chlorine when broken ; apothecia (1)-2-8 cm broad and 4-20 cm high. Sp smooth, ellipsoid, sp-print yellowish. On soil. $ Morchellaceae. $s"Morchellaceae."
13,CS16$ 13b : Apothecia with an irregular, saddle-shaped or brain-like folded ‘cap’ or cup-shaped and substipitate, 2-12 cm broad, 2-12 cm high, no smell of chlorine when broken ; stem generally shorter than ‘cap’ high. Sp with prominent appendages at the poles or smooth, ellipsoid or globose ; sp-print whitish. On soil, trunks or stumps. $ Discinaceae. $s"Discinaceae."
14,15$ 14a : Apothecia ear-shaped.
14,16$ 14b : Apothecia pulvinate, disc-shaped or cup-shaped.
15,CS17$ 15a : Apothecia blood-red to reddish brown, outside glabrous. Sp broadly ellipsoid with one large guttule. $ Helvellaceae (Wynnella). $s"Helvellaceae (Wynnella)."
15,CS20$ 15b : Apothecia yellowish, orange, ochraceous or brownish, sometimes with olivaceous, pinkish or reddish tinge. Sp narrowly ellipsoid with two small guttules. $ Pyronemataceae p.p. $s"Pyronemataceae p.p."
16,0$ 16a : Apothecia pulvinate, chestnut, 4-15 cm broad, with numerous rhizoids. Sp fusoid, apiculate, with delicate, reticulate ornamentation. On burnt ground ; autumn. $ Rhizinaceae (Rhizina).
16,17$ 16b : Apothecia not with the above combination of characters.
17,CS17$ 17a : Apothecia medium to large or if small then black, subsessile to stipitate, cup-shaped, saddle-shaped to irregularly saddle-shaped ; hymenium brownish, greyish or black ; outside glabrous or pubescent. Sp broadly ellipsoid, rarely fusoid, typically with one large guttule. On mull or calcareous soil ; spring to autumn. $ Helvellaceae p.p. $s"Helvellaceae p.p."
17,18$ 17b : Apothecia very small to medium, sessile or substipitate.
18,0$ 18a : Apothecia membranous, 3-12 mm in diam., brownish red, paler towards the margin. Excipulum of interwoven hyphae ; sp 12-15 x 5.5-7 µm, ellipsoid, smooth, without guttules. Spring to early summer. $ See Karstenellaceae (Karstenella).
18,CS20$ 18b : Apothecia not with the above combination of characters, fleshy, rarely hypogeous, pulvinate, disc-shaped or cup-shaped ; hymenium whitish, greyish, yellowish, orange, reddish, bluish or purplish ; outside glabrous, furfuraceous or hairy. Sp hyaline or pale brownish or purplish, globose, ellipsoid or fusoid, with or without guttules, smooth or ornamented. Spring to autumn. $ Pyronemataceae p.p. $s"Pyronemataceae p.p."

