13,14$ 13a : Hairs slender, like hyphae, obtuse or pointed.
13,15$ 13b : Hairs thick-walled, pointed.
14,0$ 14a : Apothecia cup-shaped, 0.2-1.5 cm broad ; hymenium orange or reddish, margin distinct, narrow ; outside with pointed, narrow, whitish hairs. Sp ellipsoid, ornamented or smooth, with guttules. On sandy soil together with Polytrichum or with Funaria on burnt ground. $ Neottiella. $s"Neottiella."
14,0$ 14b : Apothecia first hemispheric, subimmersed, then disc-shaped, 0.3-1.5 cm broad ; hymenium pale orange or dirty yellowish, margin distinct ; outside with obtuse, short, whitish hairs. Sp ellipsoid to fusoid, with guttules. On naked clayey or calcareous soil. $ Pustularia patavina.
14,0,N$ See Tarzetta.
15,0$ 15a : On dung. Hairs non-septate, thick-walled, pointed ; apothecia 0.2-1 mm broad, turbinate to disc-shaped, yellowish or reddish. Sp ellipsoid, smooth, with gelatinous coating, without guttules. $ Lasiobolus. $s"Lasiobolus."
15,0$ 15b : On dung, soil or plant debris. Hairs septate, whitish or brownish ; apothecia 0.5-1.5 cm broad, disc-shaped, margin distinct. $ Cheilymenia p.p. $s"Cheilymenia p.p."
16,17$ 16a : Hairs brownish.
16,27$ 16b : Hairs whitish.
17,0$ 17a : Hairs rooting, developing from cells in medullary excipulum. $ Scutellinia p.p. $s"Scutellinia p.p."
17,18$ 17b : Hairs developing from cells in outer excipulum.
18,0$ 18a : Apothecia first hypogeous, globose and closed, then splitting in irregular rays, cup-shaped, rarely expanding to disc-shaped, sunken, with margin at soil level ; outside covered by flexuose, brownish hairs. Sp ellipsoid, smooth, with guttules. $ Geopora. $s"Geopora."
18,19$ 18b : Apothecia epigeous.
19,20$ 19a : Hairs projecting, pointed or blunt.
19,25$ 19b : Hairs appressed, short or long, straight or flexuose.
20,0$ 20a : Sp globose. Apothecia turbinate to disc-shaped, 0.2-0.6 cm broad ; margin distinct ; hymenium brownish ; outside brown, covered by short, brownish pointed hairs. On burnt ground or clayey soil. $ Sphaerosporella. $s"Sphaerosporella."
20,21$ 20b : Sp subglobose, ellipsoid or fusoid.
21,22$ 21a : Sp with guttules.
21,23$ 21b : Sp without guttules.
22,0$ 22a : Apothecia deeply cup-shaped, rarely disc-shaped, 0.5-3 cm broad ; hymenium greyish to greyish yellow, sometimes with pink tinge ; outside covered with stiff, brownish, multiseptate hairs. Sp ellipsoid, ornamented, with two guttules. On rich soil. $ Humaria. $s"Humaria."
22,0$ 22b : Apothecia disc-shaped to low cup-shaped, 0.2-1.2 cm broad, hymenium greyish white ; outside with scattered hyaline or brownish hairs or groups of hairs. Sp subglobose, ellipsoid or fusoid, smooth or ornamented. On soil or burnt ground. $ Trichophaea. $s"Trichophaea."
23,0$ 23a : Hairs long, stiff, some bifurcate with one downward pointing branch, outer excipulum cells in vertical rows. Apothecia 0.5-3 mm broad, turbinate to disc-shaped, margin distinct ; hymenium whitish. Sp smooth. On cow-dung, soil or plant debris. $ Trichophaeopsis. $s"Trichophaeopsis."
23,24$ 23b : Hairs simple, outer excipulum cells not in rows.
24,0$ 24a : Apothecia broadly sessile, partially sunken in the substrate ; hairs only marginal. Excipulum distinctly 2-layered with relatively uniform thickness ; sp almost filling the ascus. $ Tricharina. $s"Tricharina."
24,0$ 24b : Apothecia sessile, centrally attached ; hairs marginal and all over the outside. Sp in upper half of the ascus only. $ Wilcoxina. $s"Wilcoxina."
25,0$ 25a : Sp without guttules, ellipsoid, smooth or rarely delicately ornamented. Hymenium whitish, yellowish grey or pale ochraceous to darker brownish. $ Pseudombrophila. $s"Pseudombrophila."
25,26$ 25b : Sp with guttules, ± ellipsoid, ornamented or smooth.
26,0$ 26a : Hymenium dark brown with violet tinge ; apothecia sessile, disc-shaped, 0.3-2.5 cm broad ; outside tomentose, with flexuose hairs. Sp ornamented. Usually in association with the orange Byssonectria terrestris. $ Nannfeldtiella. $s"Nannfeldtiella."
26,0$ 26b : Hymenium greyish brown or dark brownish ; apothecia 0.2-1.2 cm broad, sessile, disc-shaped, gregarious; outside with short, straight, appressed hairs. Sp smooth, with two guttules. On burnt ground. $ Anthracobia. $s"Anthracobia."
27,0$ 27a : On dung. Apothecia subimmersed, globose or subglobose, whitish or pale yellowish brown, 0.2-0.5 mm broad ; hairs stiff, pointed, 1-10 septate. With one to 3 asci each containing 1500-1700 sp. $ See Trichobolus.
27,0$ 27b : On acid soil. Apothecia superficial, disc-shaped, white all over, 0.2-1 cm broad ; hairs white, multiseptate. Asci with 8 fusoid, ornamented sp. $ Leucoscypha. $s"Leucoscypha."
28,29$ 28a : Hymenium yellowish, orange, pink or red.
28,45$ 28b : Hymenium whitish, greyish, ochraceous, brown or purplish.
29,30$ 29a : Apothecia membranous or pulvinate to disc-shaped, margin indistinct or lacking.
29,38$ 29b : Apothecia disc-shaped, cup-shaped or ear-shaped.
30,0$ 30a : On burnt ground or on otherwise sterilized soil. Apothecia densely gregarious on whitish mycelium, turbinate to pulvinate, 1-2 mm broad ; hymenium pink or reddish. Sp ellipsoid, smooth, without guttules. $ Pyronema. $s"Pyronema."
30,31$ 30b : On soil, limestone, dung, plant debris or wood.
31,32$ 31a : On soil or limestone.
31,33$ 31b : On dung, plant debris or wood.
32,0$ 32a : Sp globose or ellipsoid, with guttules ; paraphyses filiform, curved above. Apothecia 0.2-1 cm broad ; hymenium yellowish, orange or red. $ Pulvinula p.p. $s"Pulvinula p.p."
32,0$ 32b : Sp ellipsoid, with many guttules (“spumose”) ; paraphyses straight. Apothecia 1-2 mm broad ; hymenium yellowish orange. $ Kotlabaea. $s"Kotlabaea."
33,34$ 33a : On dung.
33,36$ 33b : On plant debris or wood.
34,0$ 34a : Sp ellipsoid, very delicately longitudinally striate (oil imm.) ; paraphyses stout, enlarged above to 12-15 µm broad. Apothecia 0.2-1 cm broad, hymenium orange ; outside yellowish white, furfuraceous. $ Coprobia. $s"Coprobia."
34,35$ 34b : Sp ellipsoid, smooth ; paraphyses slender, straight or curved above.
35,0$ 35a : Sp with conspicuous de Bary bubbles ; paraphyses straight, filiform or curved above. Apothecia yellow or orange, pulvinate to disc-shaped, 0.1-1 mm broad. $ Coprotus p.p. $s"Coprotus p.p."
35,0$ 35b : Sp without de Bary bubbles ; paraphyses straight, enlarged above. Apothecia yellow or yellowish brown, pulvinate to disc-shaped, 0.05-1 mm broad. $ See Thelebolus.
36,0$ 36a : Apothecia on plant debris and leaves, in places where animals urinate, in Norway, Sweden and Finland often associated with Nannfeldtiella. Apothecia gregarious on a whitish mat of mycelium (subiculum), 0.5-3 mm broad ; hymenium orange ; outside glabrous. Sp fusoid with guttules, smooth. $ Byssonectria. $s"Byssonectria."
36,37$ 36b : Apothecia on wood or soil.
37,0$ 37a : Apothecia pulvinate, 0.5-1 cm broad, yellowish, orange or ochraceous. Sp broadly ellipsoid, verruculose, with many guttules. On wet wood. $ Miladina p.p. $s"Miladina p.p."
37,0$ 37b : Apothecia thin, membranous, 0.3-1.2 cm broad, brownish red, paler towards the margin, which is orange or greenish yellow in young specimens. Sp ellipsoid, smooth without guttules. $ See Karstenella.
38,0$ 38a : Apothecia split down on one side or ear-shaped, sessile or stipitate, yellow, ochraceous, orange or pink, whitish near the pubescent base of stem. Sp ± ellipsoid, with two guttules, smooth ; paraphyses often hooked. $ Otidea p.p. $s"Otidea p.p."
38,39$ 38b : Apothecia disc-shaped or cup-shaped.
39,0$ 39a : Apothecia with ± rooting stem, fulvous, orange, yellow or greenish yellow, low cup-shaped, 0.5-5 cm broad ; stem 1-5 cm long, encrusted with debris. Sp ellipsoid, ornamented. $ Sowerbyella. $s"Sowerbyella."
39,40$ 39b : Apothecia sessile, yellowish, orange, pink or red.
40,41$ 40a : Sp globose or subglobosse.
40,42$ 40b : Sp ellipsoid or fusoid.
41,0$ 41a : Apothecia cup-shaped, 0.5-4 cm broad ; hymenium orange ; outside olivaceous orange, often blue-tinged ; greenish or bluish all over when touched. Sp smooth. $ Caloscypha. $s"Caloscypha."
41,0$ 41b : Apothecia disc-shaped, 1-2 mm broad ; hymenium yellow, orange or red, margin distinct, dentate or fimbriate. Sp ornamented. $ Lamprospora. $s"Lamprospora."
42,0$ 42a : Apothecia cup-shaped, pink, 0.5-1.2 cm broad. Sp ellipsoid, smooth, with 2 guttules. On burnt ground. $ Rhodotarzetta. $s"Rhodotarzetta."
42,43$ 42b : Apothecia yellowish, orange or reddish. On soil or mosses.
43,0$ 43a : Paraphyses above spirally curved. $ Boubovia. $s"Boubovia."
43,44$ 43b : Paraphyses above straight or slightly curved.
44,0$ 44a : Sp smooth or ornamented, with guttules. Apothecia disc-shaped or cup-shaped, 0.1-1 cm broad ; margin membranous, often dentate or fimbriate. Often together with mosses. $ Octospora. $s"Octospora."
44,0$ 44b : Sp regularly reticulate or with prominent ridges, with two guttules. Apothecia disc-shaped or deeply cup-shaped, 1-12 cm broad. Often on naked soil. $ Aleuria. $s"Aleuria."
45,46$ 45a : Apothecia globose, turbinate, pulvinate or disc-shaped.
45,55$ 45b : Apothecia cup-shaped or ear-shaped.
46,47$ 46a : On dung.
46,50$ 46b : On soil or wood.
47,0$ 47a : Sp broadly ellipsoid, subglobose or globose, with brown ornamentation. Apothecia whitish, pulvinate to globose, very simple, without excipulum cells, 0.08-0.18 mm broad. $ Ascodesmis. $s"Ascodesmis."
47,48$ 47b : Sp ellipsoid or fusoid, hyaline, smooth.
48,0$ 48a : Apothecia turbinate, white, 0.1-0.3 mm broad ; outside furfuraceous. Asci opening by a split down to an annular thickening near the apex, with 8 to 128 fusoid sp. Winter and early spring. $ See Ascozonus.
48,49$ 48b : Apothecia globose to pulvinate, white, yellow or brown, with 8 to 2500 sp in asci.
49,0$ 49a : Apothecia subglobose and subimmersed or disc-shaped and superficial, yellowish to brownish, 0.1-1 mm broad. Asci cylindric to almost globose, with 8-2500 sp, with operculum or rupturing down to a ring-like thickening below apex ; sp ellipsoid, without de Bary bubbles. $ See Thelebolus.
49,0$ 49b : Apothecia pulvinate to disc-shaped, superficial, white, greyish, yellow, orange, 0.1-1 mm broad. Asci cylindric to club-shaped, with 8-256 sp, operculate ; sp ellipsoid, with de Bary bubbles. $ Coprotus p.p. $s"Coprotus p.p."
50,51$ 50a : On soil.
50,54$ 50b : On wood.
51,52$ 51a : Apothecia white or whitish.
51,53$ 51b : Apothecia purplish or violet.
52,0$ 52a : Sp globose, smooth. Apothecia white, pulvinate to disc-shaped, 0.5-2 mm broad. $ Pulvinula p.p. $s"Pulvinula p.p."
52,0$ 52b : Sp ellipsoid, thick-walled, with semiglobose warts. Apothecia whitish, pulvinate, 0.3-0.6 mm broad. $ Chalazion. $s"Chalazion."
53,0$ 53a : Sp globose, smooth or with warts or ridges. Apothecia disc-shaped, 3-6 mm broad. $ Marcelleina. $s"Marcelleina."
53,0$ 53b : Sp ellipsoid to fusoid, coarsely ornamented with rounded warts. Apothecia disc-shaped, 0.5-2 cm broad. $ Smardaea. $s"Smardaea."
54,0$ 54a : Apothecia pulvinate, 0.5-1 cm broad, yellowish, orange or ochraceous. Sp broadly ellipsoid, verruculose, with many guttules. On wet wood (Pachyella (Pezizaceae) with non-amyloid asci will key out here). $ Miladina p.p. $s"Miladina p.p."
54,0$ 54b : Apothecia thin, membranous, 0.3-1.2 cm broad, brownish red, paler towards the margin, which is irange, or greenish yellow in young specimens. Sp ellipsoid, smooth, without guttules. $ See Karstenella.
55,0$ 55a : On burnt ground. Apothecia regular, substipitate, 0.5-1.2 cm broad, ochraceous to greyish brown, with yellowish white margin. Sp ellipsoid, smooth, without guttules. $ Geopyxis. $s"Geopyxis."
55,56$ 55b : On soil.
56,0$ 56a : Apothecia eccentric, split down on one side or ear-shaped, rarely cup-shaped, sessile or stipitate, yellow, ochraceous with pink tinge, or brownish to olivaceous, whitish near the pubescent base of the stem. Sp ellipsoid, with two guttules, smooth ; paraphyses often hooked. $ Otidea p.p. $s"Otidea p.p."
56,57$ 56b : Apothecia cup-shaped.
57,0$ 57a : Apothecia stipitate, whitish to cream, 0.5-1.5 cm broad, margin crenate ; outside furfuraceous. Asci bifurcate at the base ; sp broadly ellipsoid, smooth, when mature with one large guttules. $ Arpinia. $s"Arpinia."
57,0$ 57b : Apothecia sessile or stipitate, hymenium yellowish grey, greyish or pale ochraceous, 0.2-4 cm broad, margin crenate ; outside furfuraceous, concolorous with or slightly darker than hymenium. Sp ellipsoid, smooth, with two large guttules. $ Tarzetta p.p. $s"Tarzetta p.p."
58,0$ 58a : Sp smooth, globose. $ Stephensia. $s"Stephensia."
58,0$ 58b : Sp verrucose, broadly ellipsoid. $ Genea. $s"Genea."

CS21,0$ Sarcoscyphaceae LE GAL ex ECKBLAD

1,0$ 1a : Apothecia disc-shaped, orange, 0.2-1.2 cm broad, sessile or subsessile. Sp globose. On needles, twigs or branches of coniferous trees. $ Pithya. $s"Pithya."
1,2$ 1b : Apothecia scarlet or reddish pink. Sp large, ellipsoid. On ± buried twigs or branches of deciduous trees.
2,0$ 2a : Apothecia deeply cup-shaped, scarlet, 1-4 cm broad, stem 0-2 cm high. Asci maturing individually. $ Sarcoscypha. $s"Sarcoscypha."
2,0$ 2b : Apothecia urnulate, reddish pink, 1-1.8 cm broad, stem 2-5 cm long, rooting, sometimes branching. Asci maturing simultaneously. $ Microstoma. $s"Microstoma."

CS22,0$ Sarcosomataceae KOBAYASI

1,0$ 1a : Apothecia heavy, globose to broadly cylindric ; hymenium dark brown to black ; excipulum very thick, inside gelatinous. $ Sarcosoma. $s"Sarcosoma."
1,2$ 1b : Apothecia disc-shaped, cup-shaped or urniform ; excipulum thin, not gelatinous.
2,0$ 2a : Hymenium whitish with dark brown dots ; apothecia disc-shaped to turbinate, 1-3 mm broad. On old needles of conifers. $ Desmazierella. $s"Desmazierella."
2,3$ 2b : Hymenium dark brownish to black	3.
3,0$ 3a : Apothecia urnulate to infundibuliform, sessile or distinctly stipitate. Sp ellipsoid, hyaline, smooth, without guttules. $ Urnula. $s"Urnula."
3,4$ 3b : Apothecia cup-shaped or disc-shaped. Sp ellipsoid, ornamented and with guttules, or globose.
4,0$ 4a : Hymenium dark brown with violet tinge ; apothecia disc-shaped, 0.3-1 cm broad, outside tomentose, brown. Sp ellipsoid. In associations with the orange Byssonectria terrestris. $ See Nannfeldtiella.
4,5$ 4b : Hymenium dark brown. On needles or twigs of coniferous trees, or on Sphagnum tufts.
5,0$ 5a : Apothecia deeply cup-shaped, sessile, 0.5-1.5 cm broad ; hymenium dark brown, outside concolorous, above covered by rust red scales. Sp ellipsoid. $ Plectania. $s"Plectania."
5,0$ 5b : Apothecia cup-shaped to disc-shaped, sessile or stipitate, 0.5-6 cm broad ; hymenium dark brown to black, outside black. Sp globose. $ Pseudoplectania. $s"Pseudoplectania."

CS23,0$ Tuberaceae DUMORT.

1,0$ 1a : Asci 8-spored. Frb often large, whitish to beige. $ Choiromyces. $s"Choiromyces."
1,0$ 1b : Asci 1-4-(7)-spored. Frb often small or dark coloured. $ Tuber. $s"Tuber."

CS24,0$ Phyllachorales BARR - Phyllachoraceae THEISS. & SYD.

1,0$ 1a : Stromata elongate, 2-3 mm long, on living leaves of grasses and herbs, shining black ; perithecia erumpent through the epidermis of the leaf. $ Phyllachora. $s"Phyllachora."
1,0$ 1b : Stromata forming 2-10 mm broad, yellowish reddish areas on living leaves of trees and herbs ; perithecia developing in the darkened stroma on fallen leaves. $ Polystigma. $s"Polystigma."

CS25,0$ Rhytismatales M.E.BARR ex MINTER - Rhytismataceae CHEVALL.

1,0$ 1a : Apothecia immersed in wood of deciduous trees ; hymenium white, pruinose, exposed by rupturing of epidermis or bark. $ Propolis. $s"Propolis."
1,2$ 1b : Apothecia in dark brownish to black stroma on leaves	2.
2,0$ 2a : Only one apothecium in a dark brown, thin, circular or angular stroma, opening by many fine rays. $ Coccomyces. $s"Coccomyces."
2,0$ 2b : Several lineate, wrinkled apothecia in a common, shining, rounded, black stroma. $ Rhytisma. $s"Rhytisma."

CS26,0$ Xylariales NANNF. - Xylariaceae TUL. & C.TUL. ex WINTER

1,2$ 1a : Perithecia immersed in well developed stromata or scanted on cord- or thread-like structures ; the stromata may be greatly reduced in some species.
1,11$ 1b :  On dung or perithecia superficial, free, or immersed in plant tissues, with a subiculum or beneath a clypeus or a rudimentary stroma.
2,3$ 2a : Stromata stipitate, clavate, strap-like or thread-like, often forked especially at the apices.
2,5$ 2b : Stromata sessile, globose, hemispheric, discoid, orbicular, or effused.
3,0$ 3a : Perithecia not confined to the apex ; stromata with thin brown to black ectostromal crust, flesh remaining white or at least light coloured. $ Xylaria. $s"Xylaria."
3,4$ 3b : Perithecia clustered at the apex of stipitate stromata.
4,0$ 4a : Upper surface of stroma white or pale ; perithecia embedded in a flat disc. $ Poronia. $s"Poronia."
4,0$ 4b : Stromatal surface brown or black ; perithecia clustered at the apex of stipitate stromata which are rooted in dung or well manured soil. $ Podosordaria. $s"Podosordaria."
5,0$ 5a : Entostroma with conspicuous concentric zonation ; stromata often almost globose. $ Daldinia. $s"Daldinia."
5,6$ 5b : Entostroma not conspicuously zonate ; stromata of variable size, rarely massive.
6,0$ 6a : Entostroma white and fleshy when young, collapsing and very brittle when mature. $ Ustulina. $s"Ustulina."
6,7$ 6b : Entostroma pale brown or black when young and at maturity, not collapsing even when old.
7,0$ 7a : Entostroma pale, composed of both host and fungus tissue, perithecia seated in its base, often with long ostiole necks. $ Biscogniauxia. $s"Biscogniauxia."
7,8$ 7b : Entostroma dark brown or black or red immediately below the surface, composed of fungal tissue only, perithecia inserted in layer just below surface of stromata, ostiole necks short.
8,0$ 8a : Sp with a short, distinct, ellipsoid germ slit. $ Euepixylon. $s"Euepixylon."
8,9$ 8b : Sp with a short or long longitudinal germ slit.
9,0$ 9a : Stromata 0.5-1 cm in diam., in circular or semicircular groups in the host bark, with 1-10(-30) angularly demarcated perithecia in each stroma. $ Entoleuca. $s"Entoleuca."
9,10$ 9b : Stromata about 1 mm thick, mostly on wood, effused or in spots.
10,0$ 10a : Ascus-apparatus cylindric hat-shaped (seen in Melzer solution) ; sp with ventral germ slit if present, without loosening epispore. Frb without extractable pigments (put on 10% KOH on piece of frb on white paper) ; anamorph Geniculosporium. $ Nemania. $s"Nemania."
10,0$ 10b : Ascus-apparatus broader than high ; sp with dorsal germ slit, often with loosening epispore. Frb with extractable pigments ; anamorph Nodulosporium. $ Hypoxylon. $s"Hypoxylon."
11,12$ 11a : On dung.
11,13$ 11b : Perithecia not in or on dung.
12,0$ 12a : Stromata prominent, ( covering the dung. Sp without gelatinous coating. $ Wawelia. $s"Wawelia."
12,0$ 12b : Stromata reduced, ( immersed in the dung. Sp with a gelatinous coating. $ Hypocopra. $s"Hypocopra."
13,0$ 13a : Perithecia superficial on wood or bark, or on herbaceous stems, often on a subiculum. $ Rosellinia. $s"Rosellinia."
13,14$ 13b : Perithecia embedded in plant tissue.
14,0$ 14a : Perithecia in small clusters in bark, with convergent ostiole necks. $ Lopadostoma. $s"Lopadostoma."
14,0$ 14b : Perithecia solitary or barely coalescing, often beneath a thin clypeus. $ Anthostomella. $s"Anthostomella."
